  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Peak of winds and rain is behind us. Another \nperiod of lighter showers and southwest wind gusts are expected \nFriday afternoon while minor coastal flooding continues at high \ntides. \n\nWe're on the tail end of the peak of this holiday storm system \nas the surface cyclone continues to lift north. With it a robust\ndry slot will continue to lift north across southern New \nEngland the rest of the morning. The result is drastic cutoff in\nwidespread moderate to heavy rain as the deep moisture \nnecessary to sustain it exits. At the same time the peak of the \nwind gusts, expected to coincide with the heaviest rainfall \nwindow, is at and end as the core of the 925mb LLJ lifts into \nthe Gulf of Maine. Thus, those strong easterly winds will \nshortly become southwesterly; while a brief resurgence in wind \ngusts is possible as the dry slot arrives, the worst of the \nwinds should be behind us. \n\nThis is not to say that we're done with rain and wind by any \nmeans. Even as the best moisture and dynamics lifts out of \nsouthern New England the mid/upper level low and its surface \ncounterpart will be slow to depart. This means cyclonic flow \nwith a cool pool aloft leading to some scattered diurnal showers\nespecially over the interior. While the column will be \nrelatively drier making for some breaks in the clouds, lingering\nlow/mid level moisture will make it difficult to scour out \nclouds too much. This is before clouds thicken again late day \nand light showers fill back in a bit with a resurgence in PWAT \nas a weak shortwave rounds the trough. \n\nWhen it comes to coastal flooding, the forecast remains steady.\nThe combination of strong winds and rough seas fortunately has \nnot coincided with peak high tides and that will continue to \nminimize the coastal flood threat for Friday's high tides. We \nsaw the peak storm surge of 1.5-2 ft centered over Thanksgiving \nnight. While astro tides are higher during the Friday \nmorning/early afternoon periods, surge values look to be lower. \nFor the south coast, the gusty southwest winds during Friday \ncould make it difficult for evacuation of water out of the bays \nsupporting a continued risk for minor flooding for the Friday \nmorning high tide. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry, but blustery, conditions return for the \nweekend. \n\nLow pressure is slow to exit into the North Atlantic by \nSaturday night and SNE remains sandwiched between it and \nsouthern U.S. high pressure. The result will be continued gusty \nSW winds on Saturday, finally becoming calm on Sunday as high \npressure takes control. Moisture will also remain trapped \nbeneath a 850mb inversion on Saturday leading to continued \ncloudy skies which finally mix out by Sunday, if briefly. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chances for rain return for the start of the \nnext work week followed by more dry weather Tuesday through \nThursday.\n\nLate Sunday moisture return begins and clouds will begin to \nfill in, lowering and thickening as zonal flow becomes more \nmeridional pulling in subtropical moisture ahead of an \napproaching shortwave. This system brings a quick hitting rain \nsystem Sunday night through the first half of Monday dropping \nless than an inch of rain. Beyond Monday broad high pressure \nexpands over the whole east coast keeping the weather quiet and \nrelatively mild (upper 40s to upper 50s) through at least mid \nweek.",
